Instrument 3 -- the wire.

tools/tb tool tb277 cktac tcpflow /tmp/cktac/tac-accept.pcap --port 49

  58 packet(s) total, 4 TCP flow(s) on port 49

  flow 10.38.22.33:49 <-> 10.38.22.34:42354  packets=17  duration=0.048s  => ESTABLISHED+DATA
     10.38.22.34:42354 -> 10.38.22.33:49  pkts=8  payload=66 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN}
     10.38.22.33:49 -> 10.38.22.34:42354  pkts=9  payload=46 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN.ACK}

  flow 10.38.22.33:49 <-> 10.38.22.34:42356  packets=12  duration=0.010s  => ESTABLISHED+DATA
     10.38.22.34:42356 -> 10.38.22.33:49  pkts=6  payload=71 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN}
     10.38.22.33:49 -> 10.38.22.34:42356  pkts=6  payload=30 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN.ACK}

  (the same pair again on ports 43604 and 43614, for the second login)

  Two logins produced exactly four TCP connections, so TWO PER LOGIN.  The pair repeats
  byte for byte across both logins:

  flow           out payload  in payload  what it is
  -------------  -----------  ----------  ------------------------------------------
  first, 17 pkt  66 B         46 B        the AUTHENTICATION exchange.  An ASCII login
                                          is START, REPLY(GETPASS), CONTINUE(password),
                                          REPLY(PASS).  The client therefore sends more
                                          than it receives.  It also lasts about 5 times
                                          longer, 0.048 s, because it waits for the
                                          driver to type the password
  second, 12 pkt 71 B         30 B        the AUTHORIZATION exchange, opened right after
                                          and lasting 0.010 s.  A larger request and a
                                          much smaller reply.  This is what carries the
                                          server's "service = exec { priv-lvl = 15 }"
                                          back to the DUT

  Each connection is opened, used and closed: SYN, SYN.ACK, PSH.ACK, FIN.ACK.  The
  client does not hold one session open.

  => STEP 6 PASS: a user whose local password does not match the password that was
     offered was admitted to the DUT.  All three instruments agree the TACACS+ server
     decided.  The DUT logged "PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated".  "show
     tacacs+" moved from Unknown to Alive.  The wire carried two complete TCP port 49
     conversations per login, where the OFF baseline carried none.  This satisfies TFS
     S1599.1.010.

  A discriminator that needs no knowledge of the shared key.  The TACACS+ body is
  obfuscated, so no instrument on the wire can read the verdict.  It does not need to.
  Compare this step with STEP 7:

      outcome     TCP port 49 conversations per login
      ----------  --------------------------------------------------------------
      accepted    TWO -- the authentication exchange, then an authorization exchange
      rejected    ONE -- the authentication exchange alone, with nothing after it

  The authentication flow itself has the same shape in both cases.  It is the presence
  or absence of the SECOND flow that reveals the verdict, because the DUT only asks
  "what is this user allowed to do?" after it has been told who they are.

