  => STEP 9 PASS, with two findings.
     1. A wrong shared key has its own wire signature, and it is the opposite of a
        normal exchange.  A normal exchange is 17 packets, the client sends more than
        it receives (66 B out, 46 B in), and a second authorization flow follows.  A
        wrong key gives 12 packets, the server sends far more than it receives (41 B
        out, 72 B in), and nothing follows.  The client gets one START away and never
        reaches the CONTINUE that would carry the password.  So the password is never
        put on the wire at all when the key is wrong.
     2. A wrong shared key is treated as "server unavailable", and the "local" fallback
        DOES engage.  "manager" was admitted in 0.3 s.  Read with STEP 7, this fixes
        section 7E.1 exactly for TACACS+: a Reject is honoured and local is skipped; a
        protocol failure is not a Reject, so local is used.  The DUT also says WHY.
        platforms/x960.md section 7E.4 records that for RADIUS a wrong shared key and a
        dead server are indistinguishable in the DUT's log, because both read "failed
        to respond".  For TACACS+ the DUT prints "tac_authen_read_timeout: inconsistent
        reply body, incorrect key?", which names the cause.  STEP 11's truly unreachable
        server reads "tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed ... Transport endpoint
        is not connected" instead.  The two failure modes ARE distinguishable on TACACS+
        where they are not on RADIUS.  That follows from TACACS+ running over TCP: a
        connect failure and a decode failure happen at different layers.  The fallback
        delay here was 0.3 s, not the 25.5 s that section 7E.1 measured for an
        unreachable RADIUS server, because the TCP connection succeeded and the server
        answered at once.

  The correct key was restored immediately afterwards and the way back was re-checked:
  netlogin --proto ssh --user tacuser --password <server password> gave LOGIN RESULT:
  USER in 0.4 s, and the console was still at "awplus#".

