  --- bytes after the password was sent (THE LOGIN BANNER) ---

  AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00

  awplus>
  LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=taclow  seconds=0.5

  === CMD: show users ===
  con 0   tacuser         idle     00:00:09  ttyS0           15   10       N/A
  vty 0   taclow          idle     00:00:00  10.38.22.33     1    10       N/A

  Wire, tac-taclow.pcap: 29 packets, the two-flow accept signature.  17 packets with
  67 B out and 46 B in, then 12 packets with 72 B out and 30 B in.

  => STEP 13 PASS.  This is the case's decisive measurement.  An account that does not
     exist on the DUT in any form was admitted to its CLI, ran a command, and was listed
     by the DUT as a logged-in user.  Only the TACACS+ server can have allowed that.

  The "Last login:" discriminator.  platforms/x960.md section 7E.2 says a
  server-authenticated login prints NO "Last login:" line, because that line comes from
  the Linux lastlog of a LOCAL account.  Measured here for TACACS+:

      login                        local account?   "Last login:" in the banner?
      ---------------------------  ---------------  ----------------------------
      manager, local (STEP 1)      yes              yes
      tacuser, TACACS+ (STEPS 6,8) yes              yes
      taclow, TACACS+ (STEP 13)    no               no

  So the rule holds, and it is now sharper: the thing that decides is whether a LOCAL
  account exists, not which protocol authenticated the login.  "tacuser" was
  authenticated by TACACS+ and still printed the line.

  A second measured caveat on the same line.  "tacuser" was created in STEP 4.  Its
  very first login, seconds later, printed:

      awplus login: tacuser
      Last login: Mon Oct 19 23:00:48 UTC 2026 from 10.38.22.33

  "tacuser" had never logged in before.  It had existed for seconds, and it had never
  used an address-based access path.  So AW+ can report a "Last login:" that does not
  belong to the account shown.  A freshly created account can report a login it never
  made, from a path it never used.  Anyone using "Last login:" as a discriminator must
  know this.

  The obfuscated body is NOT padded, so the packet length leaks two lengths.  Five
  independent measurements of the client-to-server payload of the AUTHENTICATION flow
  fit one model exactly, with nothing left over:

      step  path     user (len)  password (len)       measured out  base + user + pw
      ----  -------  ----------  -------------------  ------------  ----------------
      6     console  tacuser (7) <server pw>   (10)   66 B          49 + 17
      7     console  tacuser (7) <local pw>    (10)   66 B          49 + 17
      8     ssh      tacuser (7) <server pw>   (10)   68 B          51 + 17
      10    ssh      manager (7) <password>     (6)   64 B          51 + 13
      13    ssh      taclow  (6) <server pw>   (10)   67 B          51 + 16

  The console base is 49 B and the SSH base is 51 B.  The 2-byte difference is the port
  and rem_addr attributes of the session.  Beyond that base, every byte of the username
  and every byte of the password is one byte on the wire.  TACACS+ obfuscation is a
  keystream XOR and adds no padding.  So someone watching the network who cannot read a
  single credential can still read the exact LENGTH of both the username and the
  password of every login.  This is a property of the protocol, not a defect in this
  product, but it is measured here on this product.

